[interviewer · BRAINSTORM · 11:12] Here's the thing on elasticity — I don't have that data, so you'll have to reason about it. Beyond pricing, what other levers could the client pull to restore margin? Give me a structured set. That breakdown isn't something I have. What would you ask for instead?

[candidate · 12:42] No elasticity data, so I'll assume specialty coffee is moderately inelastic — it's a habitual, low-absolute-dollar purchase and transactions grew even as baskets grew, which suggests loyal demand. I'd still phase the increase rather than take 15% at once.

Beyond price, four levers. One, procurement: renegotiate or consolidate supplier contracts across 200 stores, and hedge or forward-buy green coffee to lock pricing. Two, mix and menu engineering: steer customers toward high-margin drinks, reprice the low-margin food items that are driving basket growth, and prune the worst performers. Three, input efficiency: portion control, waste and spoilage reduction, lighter packaging, since a 38% input increase makes every gram worth more. Four, non-COGS offsets: labor scheduling and rent renegotiation — smaller here, but they protect the gains.

Instead of elasticity, I'd ask for transaction data from any store or region that has ever tested a price change, or loyalty data showing visit frequency by customer segment. A small pilot across ten stores would give us real elasticity within a quarter.
