[interviewer · BRAINSTORM · 12:44] Of pricing, procurement, mix, and efficiency, which do you pull first, and why?

[candidate · 14:14] Pricing first, clearly.

Three reasons. It's the only lever sized to the problem — my rough math says a mid-teens increase recovers roughly twelve margin points on its own, while procurement savings on a 58% COGS line might realistically deliver two or three. Second, it's the fastest: a menu price change takes weeks, whereas supplier renegotiations and hedging run on contract cycles and menu redesign takes a season. Third, we're not getting greedy — we're catching up on two years of inflation we chose to absorb, and the ticket data shows customers are spending more per visit, which signals willingness to pay.

I'd sequence it: pilot a 7 to 8% increase across twenty representative stores, read volume response over six to eight weeks, then roll out and take a second step if elasticity holds. Procurement runs in parallel since it costs nothing to start negotiating. Mix and efficiency come third — they're real but slower and smaller.

The risk is that we're the only ones raising prices, which is why I want the competitor benchmark before full rollout.
